\DOC SIMPLE_ARITH_TAC

\TYPE {SIMPLE_ARITH_TAC : tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
{SIMPLE_ARITH_TAC} attempts to prove a goal's conclusion by linear arithmetic
reasoning over the natural-number facts available in the goal, discharging the
assumptions into the problem.

\DESCRIBE
It takes no arguments. Applied to a goal, it either closes it outright or
fails:
{
   A ?- t
   ======================  SIMPLE_ARITH_TAC
}
\noindent The assumptions {A} are used as extra hypotheses; they may number in
the dozens and include non-arithmetic facts such as machine-state readings, and
assumptions irrelevant to the arithmetic are harmless. It succeeds on
conclusions built from arithmetic comparisons, equations and
conjunctions/disjunctions of them over naturals, including goals whose
conclusion is a word term or conditional whose equality follows from arithmetic
facts, and it also closes goals with no assumptions at all when the conclusion
is a valid arithmetic statement.

\FAILURE
Fails when the conclusion, taken together with whatever arithmetic content the
assumptions supply, is not derivable by the linear decision procedure it
invokes: for instance when the conclusion is plainly false as a numeric
inequality, when it depends on nonlinear facts or on non-arithmetic subterms
treated as opaque atoms, or when the assumptions needed to justify it are not
expressible in the linear fragment. The failure is reported by the arithmetic
rule the tactic calls, signalling that no contradiction could be found.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only the two outcomes of closing the goal or failing;
none shows it transforming a goal into new subgoals or altering assumptions.

\ENDDOC
